## Where live sessions are tracked

A module-private `Map<string, PtyRecord>` in `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:38`:

```ts
const ptys = new Map<string, PtyRecord>();
```

Each `PtyRecord` (defined at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:21-36`) holds the actual `node-pty` handle (`proc`), `cwd`, `owner` (the renderer `WebContents`), `kind` (`'agent' | 'shell' | 'tui' | 'service'`), `taskId`/`featureId`, and a headless `TerminalMirror` used to replay output to a reattaching renderer. All session lifecycle functions (`startDirectPty`, `startPty`, `startCommandPty`, `killPty`, `resizePty`, `listForTask`, etc.) key off this single map — there's no per-kind registry.

## How resize is applied

`resizePty(id, cols, rows)` at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:695-705`:

```ts
export function resizePty(id: string, cols: number, rows: number): void {
  const record = ptys.get(id);
  if (record) {
    record.mirror?.resize(cols, rows);
    try {
      record.proc.resize(cols, rows);
    } catch {
      // EBADF can happen during transitions
    }
  }
}
```

It looks up the record, resizes the headless `TerminalMirror` (so the serialized-for-reattach state matches), then calls `node-pty`'s own `proc.resize(cols, rows)`, wrapped in a try/catch because an `EBADF` can occur if the resize races a PTY that's mid-teardown.

## How a process is shut down, and the timeout

Graceful two-stage kill in `gracefulKillProc` at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:727-763`, with the grace window defined at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:708`:

```ts
const GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000;
```

The function sends `SIGTERM` first, and starts a timer for `GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS` (3000 ms / 3 seconds). If the process exits before the timer fires (via `proc.onExit`), the promise resolves immediately. If not, the timer fires `proc.kill('SIGKILL')` to force it. The comment at `src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:715-718` explains why: node-pty's bare `kill()` sends `SIGHUP`, which Claude Code doesn't trap, so the in-memory session tail was being lost on refresh/quit — SIGTERM gives Claude a chance to flush its session to disk first.

This is invoked by:
- `killPty(id)` / `killPtyAwait(id)` → `killPtyInternal` (`ptyManager.ts:777-801`), single PTY
- `killAll()` (`ptyManager.ts:808-818`), all PTYs in parallel on app quit — bounded to ~one grace window total, not the sum
- `killByOwner(owner)` (`ptyManager.ts:825-832`), all PTYs owned by a closing window

## What's special about the spawn environment

Built in `buildDirectEnv(isDark, cwd)` (`src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:231-340`), used for agent (Claude) PTYs via `startDirectPty` (`ptyManager.ts:502-509`):

- **Minimal by default**: unless `syncShellEnv` is toggled on, the base env is `{}` rather than inheriting `process.env` (`ptyManager.ts:233-235`) — for "fast, predictable spawns."
- `TERM_PROGRAM: 'dash'`, `HOME`, a merged `PATH` (prepends a Dash-managed `rtk` bin dir if present), and `COLORFGBG` encoding light/dark terminal background for CLI apps that honor the rxvt convention (`ptyManager.ts:244-252`).
- Platform split: Windows gets `USERNAME` plus a curated list of system vars (`APPDATA`, `SystemRoot`/`SYSTEMROOT`, `COMSPEC`, etc., `ptyManager.ts:258-277`); POSIX gets `TERM=xterm-256color`, `COLORTERM=truecolor`, `USER` (`ptyManager.ts:279-281`).
- **Auth passthrough** when not syncing full shell env: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN`, and proxy vars are explicitly forwarded (`ptyManager.ts:286-303`).
- User-configured env vars are merged in, but blocked from overriding a `RESERVED_ENV_KEYS` set (`PATH`, `HOME`, `USER`, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `COLORFGBG`, `DASH_HOOK_PORT` — `ptyManager.ts:127-138`).
- Per-task port env vars from `WorkspacePortsRuntime.getEnvForWorktree(cwd)` are merged so commands the agent runs resolve the same ports shown in Dash's UI (`ptyManager.ts:320-324`).
- `CLAUDE_CODE_NO_FLICKER=1` disables Claude Code's built-in viewport scrolling since Dash renders its own terminal viewport (`ptyManager.ts:327`).
- `DASH_HOOK_PORT` is set last (winning over any inherited value) only if the in-process `HookServer` is actually bound, so hook scripts' `[ -n … ]` guard no-ops instead of erroring when a session was launched outside Dash (`ptyManager.ts:329-337`).

Separately, plain shell PTYs (`startPty`, `ptyManager.ts:563-669`) *do* inherit full `process.env`, minus Electron packaging artifacts (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE`, `ELECTRON_NO_ATTACH_CONSOLE`, `ptyManager.ts:591-594`), spawn with `-i` (interactive, non-login) rather than `-il` to skip ~0.5s of login-file sourcing since PATH is already fixed up at app boot (`ptyManager.ts:586-588`), and set `ZDOTDIR` to a Dash-managed dir to inject a custom zsh prompt (`ptyManager.ts:602-605`).
